course: euphemisms
lesson: 2
title: Soft words for hard things: death and dying
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Not every euphemism for death is meant to be kind; some are meant to hide or to sound tough.
explain: Hospitals, armies and governments use cold official words, such as "negative patient outcome" or "collateral damage", so that a death sounds like a number in a report instead of a person.
explain: Gangsters in films use slang such as "whacked" or "sleeping with the fishes" to talk about murder without saying the word.
explain: Every country also has its own jokey words: an English person "popped his clogs", an Australian "carked it", an American "cashed in his chips".
explain: Vivid example: a general who says "the target was neutralized" means that people were killed, but the words make it sound like switching off a machine.
fact: "Loved one", in the speech of funeral companies, means the dead person.
fact: "The departed" means the dead person or the dead people; it is polite.
fact: "Negative patient outcome" is American hospital jargon for "the patient died".
fact: "Collateral damage" is official military language for ordinary people killed or property destroyed by accident in an attack.
fact: "Neutralized" in official military or police talk means killed.
fact: "Taken out" in slang means killed on purpose.
fact: "Whacked" is American gangster slang for murdered.
fact: "Sleeping with the fishes" is American gangster slang for murdered and dumped in water.
fact: "Slipped away" means died quietly and peacefully.
fact: "Gave up the ghost" means died; it is also said jokingly of a machine that stops working for good.
fact: "Popped his clogs" means died; it is humorous British English.
fact: "Carked it" means died; it is Australian slang.
fact: "Cashed in his chips" means died; it is American slang taken from card games.
fact: "Went west" means died or was destroyed; it is old-fashioned British English.
fact: "Lost her battle with cancer" means she died of cancer.
fact: "Succumbed to his injuries" means he died of his injuries; it is used in official reports and the news.
fact: "Didn't make it" means died, usually after an accident or illness.
fact: "Expired" means died; it is medical or official jargon (a ticket or a carton of milk can also "expire").
fact: "Crossed over" means died; it is a gentle, often spiritual phrase.
fact: "Called home" means died; it is used in American religious talk, meaning called home to God.
exercise: A funeral company's leaflet says "We take care of your loved one with respect." Who is the "loved one"? | The dead person | The family's pet | The person paying the bill | The priest at the funeral
exercise: "A prayer for the departed." Who are "the departed"? | The dead | Travellers | People who moved away | Passengers leaving the airport
exercise: What does "negative patient outcome" mean in a hospital report? | The patient died | The patient was unhappy | The test result was negative | The patient went home early
exercise: An army spokesman says there was "some collateral damage". What really happened? | Ordinary people were killed or homes destroyed | Some army trucks were scratched | The army lost some money | Some soldiers got lost
exercise: "The gunman was neutralized by police." What happened to the gunman? | He was killed | He was calmed down by talking | He was drugged | He was made to sit still
exercise: In a spy film, "the boss wants him taken out". What does the boss want? | Him killed | Him fed | Him taken out of the room | Him taken on a trip
exercise: In a gangster film, "Tony got whacked last night." What happened to Tony? | He was murdered | He was slapped once | He was hit by a ball | He was told off
exercise: "Joey's sleeping with the fishes now." In a gangster film this means what? | Joey was murdered and thrown in the water | Joey went fishing | Joey sleeps by his fish tank | Joey lives on a boat
exercise: "Grandma slipped away in the night." What does that mean? | She died quietly in the night | She sneaked out in the night | She fell over in the night | She left the house in secret
exercise: "My old laptop finally gave up the ghost." What happened to the laptop? | It stopped working for good | It got a virus | It showed a scary picture | It went to sleep mode
exercise: A British man says "When I pop my clogs, the house is yours." When will you get the house? | When he dies | When he buys new shoes | When he dances | When he moves out
exercise: An Australian says "the old dog carked it yesterday". What happened? | The dog died | The dog barked all day | It ran off | The dog got sick
exercise: "The old gambler cashed in his chips last year." What happened? | He died | He won a lot of money | He quit | He sold his casino
exercise: An old British soldier says "half the regiment went west at Ypres." (A regiment is a big group of soldiers; Ypres is a town where a First World War battle was fought.) What does he mean? | Half the regiment were killed | Half the regiment marched west | Half the regiment went home | Half the regiment got lost
exercise: "The actor lost his battle with cancer." What happened to him? | He died of cancer | He beat cancer | He played a fighter in a film | He lost a fight with a rival
exercise: "The driver succumbed to his injuries in hospital." What does this mean? | He died of his injuries | He recovered from his injuries | He was sent home with bandages | He refused treatment
exercise: "The firefighters tried, but the man didn't make it." What happened to the man? | He died | He missed his bus | He did not finish a race | He was late
exercise: A hospital note says "patient expired at 3:15." What happened at 3:15? | The patient died | The patient's insurance ended | She breathed out | The patient was sent home
exercise: "His wife crossed over last spring." What does "crossed over" mean? | Died | Crossed the road | Moved | Changed teams
exercise: At an American church: "Sister Ruth was called home on Tuesday." What happened? | Sister Ruth died | Sister Ruth was phoned at home | Sister Ruth went back home | Sister Ruth retired
test: News: "Two people were injured in the crash. One later succumbed in hospital." How many people died? | One | None | Two | Three
test: Report: "The strike hit the bridge. Collateral damage was limited." What is the report trying not to say plainly? | That some ordinary people were hurt or killed | That the bridge was not hit | That the weather was bad | That the soldiers were tired
test: Chatty: "Mate, the telly's given up the ghost again, I'm not paying for another." What is the speaker angry about? | The TV broke | The TV showed a ghost film | A ghost broke the TV | It was stolen
test: Liam says "the hospital said Gran had a negative outcome, so she's fine, right?" What is true? | No, it means she died | Yes, a negative test means she is fine | Yes, it means she can go home | No, it means she needs more tests
test: A tough guy in a film: "Touch my car again and you'll be sleeping with the fishes." What kind of remark is this? | A threat to kill, in gangster slang | An invitation to go fishing | A joke about a fish tank | A promise of a holiday
test: Kate's mum says "Uncle Pat popped his clogs." Kate asks "did his shoes break?" What should Kate be told? | No, it means Uncle Pat died | Yes, his clogs burst | No, it means he went dancing | Yes, he needs new shoes
test: A doctor tells a family "I'm so sorry, she didn't make it." What should the family understand? | She has died | She missed her appointment | She is asleep | She failed her operation test
test: Gossip: "Old Mick finally carked it, the stubborn old goat." What do you learn? | Mick died, and the speaker is being rough about it | Mick's goat died | Mick lost an argument | Mick is very ill
test: Funeral leaflet: "Choose a coffin for your loved one." Who will lie in the coffin? | The dead person | The buyer | The child of the family | The funeral director
test: A milk carton says "expired 3 May". What is the difference from "the patient expired"? | The milk went off, but the patient died | Both mean something died | Both mean something went off | The milk died, but the patient went off
test: A police statement: "The suspect was neutralized at the scene." Which plain sentence matches? | The police killed the suspect there | The police arrested the suspect gently | The suspect ran away | The suspect gave up and sat down
test: "He went west in 1917," says Grandad about his own grandad, a soldier. Where is that soldier now? | He died in 1917 | He lives in the west | He moved to America in 1917 | He walked home in 1917
exam: Identify the plain meaning of the term "collateral damage" as it appears in an official military communiqué. | The unintended killing of civilians or destruction of property | Damage sustained by military vehicles in transit | Financial losses incurred by the armed forces | Injuries sustained by soldiers during training
exam: It is given that a hospital record states that a patient "expired". Ascertain what occurred. | The patient died | The patient's registration lapsed | The patient exhaled deeply | The patient left
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of "succumbed to his injuries" in a press report. | He died as a consequence of his injuries | He recovered fully from his injuries | He declined medical treatment for his injuries | He sustained further injuries
exam: Determine which of the following expressions denotes death in the humorous register of British English. | Popped his clogs | Carked it | Called home | Negative patient outcome
